    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters August 15 with a lineup optimized for high-volume production, headlined by the slate\u2019s elite play: **Corbin Carroll**. Sporting an 82.38 score, **Corbin Carroll** is the cornerstone of our strategy today against Grant Holmes; while the matchup carries a 16% SP skill advantage, his 60% stolen base probability against an elite hold pitcher makes him the engine of our pursuit of that elusive scarcity category. Similarly, **Josh Naylor** (72.72) and **Seiya Suzuki** (68.79) are must-starts. **Josh Naylor** benefits significantly from the matchup against Wesneski, where a 19% SP skill advantage and a high-leverage 5-hole spot in the order elevate his baseline to a stellar efficiency rating. **Mickey Moniak** also demands attention with a 65.84 score, buoyed by a substantial 14% boost from a favorable righty-righty platoon matchup that makes him one of our most efficient leverage points today.\n\nThe marginal decisions remain fluid as we wait for final verification on lineups. **Brice Turang** (66.26) is a critical component of our stolen base strategy, as his matchup against Wrobleski presents an 85% probability for swipes; the 5% wind-out factor in his park pushes his score comfortably into our \"Strong\" tier. Conversely, **Travis Bazzana** was our most difficult cut. Despite a decent 45.07 projection, he was held out of the starting nine because he currently lacks the efficiency ceiling offered by the rest of our outfield and middle infield depth. We are closely monitoring the status of **Max Muncy**, **Cole Young**, and **Austin Riley**\u2014all are currently slated in our \"Lineup Pending\" assumptions. Should **Austin Riley** slide further down the order than our #8 projection, his score will drop, but for now, his massive 17% SP skill advantage keeps him in the starting mix despite the order-based penalty. \n\nFinally, remain alert for late-breaking news regarding our bench and injury reserve. While we are currently optimized, the absence of **James Wood** and **Trevor Story** to IL stints continues to limit our ceiling, forcing us to rely on our active roster depth rather than top-tier bench assets. We are currently cycling through **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, **Zach McKinstry**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Edwin Arroyo**, and **Henry Bolte** to ensure we aren't leaving production on the table as we manage our game caps. Any weather-related shifts in the park factors for **Max Muncy** or **Tyler Soderstrom**\u2014who both share a tough matchup against MacKenzie Gore\u2014could necessitate a pivot to **Travis Bazzana** if a pinch-hit opportunity arises.",
